WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.080
Instructor: Alright, so next up

00:01.080 --> 00:04.200
I'd like to show another example of resolving conflicts

00:04.200 --> 00:07.950
but this time I'm gonna show you how to do it using VS Code.

00:07.950 --> 00:10.410
So VS Code has some nice built-in functionality

00:10.410 --> 00:12.240
to help you resolve conflicts

00:12.240 --> 00:14.340
or it can help in some scenarios.

00:14.340 --> 00:17.010
So I'm just going to come up with another situation

00:17.010 --> 00:18.960
that will cause a merge conflict.

00:18.960 --> 00:21.600
What I'm gonna do is go to my master branch,

00:21.600 --> 00:23.100
which I'm currently on.

00:23.100 --> 00:24.900
We'll ignore the combo, the serena,

00:24.900 --> 00:26.310
the bjorn branches for now.

00:26.310 --> 00:27.810
I'm gonna go to the master branch

00:27.810 --> 00:30.210
and I'm gonna make a new branch from here.

00:30.210 --> 00:33.000
So currently on master, we just have four ABBA songs

00:33.000 --> 00:36.360
and three podcasts in the podcast file.

00:36.360 --> 00:38.040
So I'm gonna make a new branch.

00:38.040 --> 00:40.050
Let's do git switch. You know what?

00:40.050 --> 00:43.440
Why don't I, just to be different,

00:43.440 --> 00:46.320
I'll use git checkout -b.

00:46.320 --> 00:48.900
If you remember, this is another way

00:48.900 --> 00:51.000
of making a branch and switching to it.

00:51.000 --> 00:53.670
It's the old way of doing it before git switch.

00:53.670 --> 00:57.210
It's -b to make the branch and switch to it.

00:57.210 --> 00:58.680
It's kind of confusing.

00:58.680 --> 01:02.130
The alternative is git switch -c.

01:02.130 --> 01:04.590
Anyway, I might be screwing you over

01:04.590 --> 01:06.660
by mixing and matching them.

01:06.660 --> 01:07.920
I don't know, maybe I'm...

01:07.920 --> 01:10.200
I'm telling myself it's better to expose you to them

01:10.200 --> 01:12.030
so that you see the different options.

01:12.030 --> 01:14.310
But maybe it's just confusing. I apologize.

01:14.310 --> 01:17.540
Anyway, this branch is going to be called...

01:18.480 --> 01:21.243
How about, I don't know, silence.

01:22.290 --> 01:26.160
Okay? So git checkout -b silence.

01:26.160 --> 01:31.160
And this playlist, the person who wants silence,

01:31.170 --> 01:35.580
they're going to go to their playlist and remove everything,

01:35.580 --> 01:40.580
all songs and all podcasts like that.

01:40.950 --> 01:43.860
And they're going to add and commit. So git add.

01:43.860 --> 01:46.263
Let's just add dot, git commit,

01:48.930 --> 01:53.930
empty podcasts and songs files.

01:55.110 --> 01:58.710
Alright, so that's our first half, right?

01:58.710 --> 02:00.360
We have our branch silence

02:00.360 --> 02:03.360
that we basically just emptied everything out of.

02:03.360 --> 02:04.920
We still have the files though.

02:04.920 --> 02:09.450
Then I'm gonna switch back to master. git switch master.

02:09.450 --> 02:13.650
And what should we do? Let's add a podcast.

02:13.650 --> 02:16.920
So The Lead, Radiolab, this American Life.

02:16.920 --> 02:19.770
One I liked recently is Recipe Club

02:19.770 --> 02:23.850
and another one is Broken Record, a great music podcast.

02:23.850 --> 02:26.340
And then we'll add a song in here.

02:26.340 --> 02:27.720
Should we do another ABBA song?

02:27.720 --> 02:30.093
How about "Waterloo" by ABBA?

02:30.930 --> 02:33.960
Alright, so I'm gonna add and commit those.

02:33.960 --> 02:36.300
git add ., git commit.

02:36.300 --> 02:39.360
Let's just go with, I don't know, something generic.

02:39.360 --> 02:41.760
Add podcasts and songs.

02:41.760 --> 02:44.670
It's not a very, it's not a good commit message at all

02:44.670 --> 02:47.700
but just keeping it quick and simple.

02:47.700 --> 02:50.550
Okay, so this is where we stand now.

02:50.550 --> 02:54.000
On my master branch, I have this new commit,

02:54.000 --> 02:55.530
add podcasts and songs, right?

02:55.530 --> 02:58.620
I added this right here, "Waterloo" by ABBA

02:58.620 --> 03:00.660
and these two podcasts.

03:00.660 --> 03:03.600
But before I did that, I branched off

03:03.600 --> 03:06.120
and that's where we created our branch called silence.

03:06.120 --> 03:09.270
And on the silence branch I did one thing

03:09.270 --> 03:13.290
and one thing only, which was I emptied out those two files.

03:13.290 --> 03:15.330
So I have a new commit on silence

03:15.330 --> 03:17.430
and I have a new commit on master.

03:17.430 --> 03:19.350
I'm going to try and merge them.

03:19.350 --> 03:22.290
And as we know, that should generate a conflict

03:22.290 --> 03:24.900
because it's not as simple as a fast forward

03:24.900 --> 03:28.440
and Git can't figure out automatically how to merge these

03:28.440 --> 03:33.120
because we edited this podcast file, both on this branch

03:33.120 --> 03:35.490
and on the silence branch.

03:35.490 --> 03:37.800
And we did the same thing for the songs file.

03:37.800 --> 03:41.700
So if I wanna merge in the silence branch to master

03:41.700 --> 03:44.640
I'll check git status. Okay, we're good to go.

03:44.640 --> 03:46.533
git merge silence.

03:47.790 --> 03:49.710
And now we have an example

03:49.710 --> 03:51.810
where we have two files with conflicts.

03:51.810 --> 03:53.610
So that's another thing I wanted to demonstrate.

03:53.610 --> 03:55.710
You're not always just gonna get one file.

03:56.580 --> 03:58.740
Sometimes you can get a whole bunch of files,

03:58.740 --> 04:00.450
especially if...

04:00.450 --> 04:01.890
Well, we can address that later.

04:01.890 --> 04:03.270
If things have gone wrong

04:03.270 --> 04:07.350
or you're not really getting your coworkers' code

04:07.350 --> 04:09.060
as frequently as you should.

04:09.060 --> 04:12.090
Let's say, you do a whole bunch of work for a year

04:12.090 --> 04:13.740
and then you come back to the office

04:13.740 --> 04:15.720
and you try and merge things into master,

04:15.720 --> 04:17.280
it's not gonna go well.

04:17.280 --> 04:21.450
Anyway, two files with conflicts, songs and podcasts.

04:21.450 --> 04:25.230
Automatic merge failed. Fix them and then add and commit.

04:25.230 --> 04:27.990
So I wanted to show how to do this with VS Code

04:27.990 --> 04:31.020
where there's a built-in interface.

04:31.020 --> 04:35.190
So most editors, code editors have some support for this.

04:35.190 --> 04:39.510
This is really nice if you have to resolve conflicts.

04:39.510 --> 04:42.450
We don't have to manually go and delete everything, right?

04:42.450 --> 04:45.180
If I want to accept silence,

04:45.180 --> 04:47.220
if that's what I want on this branch,

04:47.220 --> 04:48.960
I merge in the changes from silence

04:48.960 --> 04:53.610
and I want the silence version, so the empty files,

04:53.610 --> 04:56.730
I can click accept incoming change.

04:56.730 --> 04:58.230
This is the incoming change.

04:58.230 --> 04:59.880
Or I can accept the current change,

04:59.880 --> 05:01.680
what we already have on master.

05:01.680 --> 05:03.390
Or I can accept both changes,

05:03.390 --> 05:04.800
which it's not gonna look like anything

05:04.800 --> 05:08.520
because silence, as you can see, there's nothing here.

05:08.520 --> 05:10.080
We deleted it, right?

05:10.080 --> 05:12.870
On that branch we committed an empty file.

05:12.870 --> 05:15.840
So in each of these files where there's a conflict

05:15.840 --> 05:19.200
I could go about it manually, delete these markers myself,

05:19.200 --> 05:24.200
choose what I want, or I can just accept incoming change.

05:24.480 --> 05:26.400
So it does it for me, not that impressive

05:26.400 --> 05:28.770
because it just deletes what was in there.

05:28.770 --> 05:31.020
But that's only because our incoming branch was empty

05:31.020 --> 05:31.853
in this file.

05:32.880 --> 05:34.080
So I could do that.

05:34.080 --> 05:36.510
Or if I wanted to, I can put anything in here.

05:36.510 --> 05:40.350
Just I can add changes in here, silence,

05:40.350 --> 05:42.000
and save that and add and commit.

05:42.000 --> 05:45.420
It's totally up to me. I'm not limited to just either or.

05:45.420 --> 05:47.130
Sometimes when you're resolving conflicts,

05:47.130 --> 05:49.860
you may need to go in and add additional code

05:49.860 --> 05:52.620
or a comment to explain something.

05:52.620 --> 05:54.540
So it's not always just a binary.

05:54.540 --> 05:57.840
This one, that one. Sometimes there's a bit of a gray area.

05:57.840 --> 06:00.060
So I'm gonna accept the incoming change again,

06:00.060 --> 06:01.050
or maybe I'll just show you.

06:01.050 --> 06:02.850
If you clicked accept current change

06:03.990 --> 06:06.210
we end up with what we currently had on master

06:06.210 --> 06:08.370
but I'm gonna delete that.

06:08.370 --> 06:10.830
We've now gone to silence in both of them.

06:10.830 --> 06:12.510
We merged in those changes

06:12.510 --> 06:15.450
that was coming from our silence branch

06:15.450 --> 06:17.673
and now I can go over to my terminal.

06:18.600 --> 06:21.210
Oh, by the way, here the GUI also shows me

06:21.210 --> 06:22.383
that there's conflicts,

06:23.700 --> 06:25.530
and specifically it would even show me

06:25.530 --> 06:28.740
where the conflicts are and I guess I can resolve them here.

06:28.740 --> 06:30.510
I've never used GitKraken to do that,

06:30.510 --> 06:32.580
but that's kind of cool.

06:32.580 --> 06:37.580
Anyway, I'm gonna go to my terminal and git status.

06:38.130 --> 06:39.960
We have those two files we modified

06:39.960 --> 06:42.900
so I'm gonna add them and git commit

06:42.900 --> 06:46.413
and just do something like merge silence branch.

06:47.790 --> 06:49.380
And there we go.

06:49.380 --> 06:51.000
Now we take a look at the GUI

06:51.000 --> 06:54.540
and we can see we merge that silence branch into master.

06:54.540 --> 06:57.780
And with that merge it doesn't look like much

06:57.780 --> 07:00.030
because our files are both empty.

07:00.030 --> 07:02.480
But that did indeed come from the silence branch.

07:03.540 --> 07:05.250
We still have this whole history on master,

07:05.250 --> 07:07.317
a whole bunch of commits that came before it.

07:07.317 --> 07:09.510
You can see that with git log, right?

07:09.510 --> 07:12.930
All these commits and then we have merge silence branch.

07:12.930 --> 07:15.510
And we can see empty podcasts and songs files.

07:15.510 --> 07:16.650
That is the commit

07:16.650 --> 07:19.470
that came from the silence branch right there.

07:19.470 --> 07:22.350
Okay, so that's just another example of resolving conflicts.

07:22.350 --> 07:24.810
In this case, we had two files that we resolved.

07:24.810 --> 07:27.870
You can have, I mean, as many as there are.

07:27.870 --> 07:30.630
There's no limit on that and we fix 'em all in the same way.

07:30.630 --> 07:33.060
You open each one up, figure out what you want to keep,

07:33.060 --> 07:34.560
keep the incoming branch changes,

07:34.560 --> 07:36.570
the current branch changes, or both,

07:36.570 --> 07:37.740
save your files

07:37.740 --> 07:40.863
and then make that new commit to merge them, okay.
